~ core.process holds the signal trap's vocabulary: `Sender` and `Signal`.
~ A trap's arms (`!>`) name `Signal`'s variants bare — `Interrupt`, `Terminate`, and so on.

~ What the OS reports about a signal's sender: the sending process's id, and the real user
~ id it ran as. A signal sent from outside any process — a terminal hangup, an alarm — has
~ pid 0.
>> Sender = { pid :: Num, uid :: Num }

~ Every signal a program can trap, one variant per signal, each carrying its `Sender`. A
~ trap's arm not written for a variant here keeps the OS default for that signal.
>> Signal = Hangup(Sender)       ~ SIGHUP
  / Interrupt(Sender)            ~ SIGINT
  / Quit(Sender)                 ~ SIGQUIT
  / Terminate(Sender)            ~ SIGTERM
  / Alarm(Sender)                ~ SIGALRM
  / UserDefined1(Sender)         ~ SIGUSR1
  / UserDefined2(Sender)         ~ SIGUSR2
